# Every setting the dashboard reads, with its default. For `pnpm dev`, copy
# this file to apps/dashboard/.env.local. For Docker Compose, put the same
# lines in the repo-root .env instead: compose passes every variable in it to
# the dashboard container. Leave an optional setting out (or commented)
# rather than empty: an empty value makes a few numeric knobs misbehave.

# Local Postgres started by `docker compose up postgres` at repo root.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://crumb:crumb@localhost:5432/crumb

# Tip: any var below also accepts a `<VAR>_FILE` companion pointing at a file
# whose contents are the value (Docker/Kubernetes secrets convention). The
# Docker entrypoint loads it at startup unless the var is set directly —
# e.g. CRUMB_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/enc_key. See the README "Secrets from files".

# ─── Tier ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "self_host" (default): OSS deployment. AGPL. Free. Email goes to stdout.
#                        Integrations work if you BYO OAuth-app creds.
#                        AI + Session Record are Cloud-only (off here).
# "cloud":               the hosted tier at crumb-app.localhostlabs.net. Enables managed
#                        email + makes AI / Session Record / integrations
#                        available — gated per-workspace by Stripe plan
#                        (see lib/entitlements.ts).
# CRUMB_TIER=self_host

# ─── Public origin ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonical public origin of this dashboard. When set, it's used to build every
# OAuth integration callback (`{CRUMB_APP_URL}/api/integrations/<provider>/callback`)
# instead of guessing from x-forwarded-host — the recommended single knob behind
# a proxy / load balancer. Precedence: CRUMB_APP_URL → per-provider *_REDIRECT_URL
# → request host. Register the matching callback URL in each provider's app.
# CRUMB_APP_URL=https://app.yourdomain.com

# ─── Secret encryption at rest ──────────────────────────────
# Encrypts integration tokens and chat webhook URLs with AES-256-GCM before
# they're written to the database. Strongly recommended for Cloud; optional
# for single-node self-host. When unset, they're stored in plaintext (a
# startup warning is logged).
#
# Value is 32 bytes: 64 hex chars or base64. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# After setting it on an existing deployment, encrypt the Slack, Linear and
# Jira tokens already stored (reconnect any other integration to encrypt its
# secret):
#   docker compose exec dashboard node packages/db/dist/backfill-encrypt-secrets.mjs
#   or, from a checkout: pnpm --filter @crumb/db exec tsx src/backfill-encrypt-secrets.ts
#
# Rotation: set the new key as CRUMB_ENCRYPTION_KEY, bump CRUMB_ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID,
# and add the prior key as CRUMB_ENCRYPTION_KEY_<oldId> (e.g.
# CRUMB_ENCRYPTION_KEY_1). New writes use the new key. Values already
# encrypted stay under the old one until they're next written (a reconnect or
# token refresh); the backfill skips them, so keep the old key set.
# CRUMB_ENCRYPTION_KEY=...
# CRUMB_ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID=1

# ─── Logging & error tracking ───────────────────────────────
# Structured JSON logs go to stdout/stderr. Level: debug | info | warn |
# error. Defaults to "info" in production, "debug" otherwise.
# CRUMB_LOG_LEVEL=info
#
# Optional error tracking: log.error() forwards exceptions to Sentry. Needs
# @sentry/node, which Crumb doesn't ship (it isn't in the Docker image), so
# add it to your own build. Without it a set DSN only logs a warning.
# SENTRY_DSN=https://...ingest.sentry.io/...
#
# Logs server timings for each thread page (session vs. data load), to tell
# database latency from rendering cost. Off when unset.
# CRUMB_PERF_LOG=1

# ─── Storage ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where attachment + Session Record bytes live.
#   "local" (default for pnpm dev): files under CRUMB_STORAGE_DIR (default
#               .crumb-uploads in the dashboard's working directory). Inside
#               a container that disk is thrown away on every recreate, so
#               docker-compose.yml defaults to "postgres". In Docker, use
#               local only with CRUMB_STORAGE_DIR on a mounted volume, and back
#               that directory up with the database.
#   "postgres": bytes stored in the DB (base64 text, about a third larger
#               than the file). Durable, shared by every instance, and part of
#               every pg_dump. Fine at the 10 MB upload cap, but it grows the
#               database. Required for multi-instance Cloud.
# CRUMB_STORAGE_PROVIDER=local
# CRUMB_STORAGE_DIR=/data/crumb-uploads

# ─── Rate limiting & scaling ────────────────────────────────
# Public-API POSTs are rate-limited per source IP with a token bucket:
# CRUMB_RATE_LIMIT_CAPACITY=60          # tokens per bucket
# CRUMB_RATE_LIMIT_REFILL_PER_SEC=1     # tokens per second
#
# When set, the rate limiter uses a shared Redis token bucket instead of a
# per-process in-memory one, which you need with more than one app instance
# behind a load balancer (otherwise each box enforces its own copy of the
# limit). Needs ioredis, which Crumb doesn't ship (it isn't in the Docker
# image); without it the limiter logs an error and stays in-memory. Unset =
# the in-memory limiter, correct for single-node self-host. Also falls back
# to in-memory if Redis is unreachable.
# CRUMB_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ─── Email delivery ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Default is "stdout" — magic-link emails print to the dashboard's log.
# Fine for dev / first-run; not deliverable.
#
# SMTP relay (any tier: Postmark, SendGrid, SES, Mailgun, Postfix, ...):
# CRUMB_EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.postmarkapp.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=true                      # only with SMTP_PORT=465 (TLS from the first byte)
# SMTP_USER=...
# SMTP_PASS=...
# CRUMB_EMAIL_FROM="Crumb <crumb@yourdomain.com>"
#
# Managed email via Resend requires CRUMB_TIER=cloud:
# CRUMB_EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# CRUMB_EMAIL_FROM="Crumb <crumb@yourdomain.com>"
#
# Operator address told about every completed self-serve signup (Cloud), and
# where in-app Help → Contact messages go (CRUMB_SUPPORT_EMAIL wins when set).
# With neither, or no real email provider, the contact form is hidden.
# CRUMB_OPS_EMAIL=ops@yourdomain.com
# CRUMB_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@yourdomain.com

# ─── Slack integration (vendor notifications) ───────────────
# Workspace admins click "Connect Slack" in Settings → Integrations to
# install. Customer replies on items they own (or all admins, if
# unassigned) then DM the right teammate when they pick "Slack" as their
# delivery channel.
#
# Cloud: we hold creds; self-host: register your own app at
# https://api.slack.com/apps with bot scopes (the list in lib/slack/install.ts,
# also shown in Settings → Integrations' setup details):
#   chat:write, im:write, users:read, users:read.email, app_mentions:read, commands
# and set the OAuth redirect URL to
#   {dashboard origin}/api/integrations/slack/callback
# SLACK_CLIENT_ID=...
# SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=...
# Optional override when host detection is unreliable (e.g. behind a tunnel):
# SLACK_REDIRECT_URL=https://your-dashboard.example.com/api/integrations/slack/callback
# (The OAuth state key is CRUMB_OAUTH_STATE_SECRET, shared by every provider:
# see the next section.)
# /crumb slash command (create a Crumb item from Slack): with the "commands"
# scope above, point the command + interactivity Request URLs at
#   {origin}/api/integrations/slack/commands  and  .../slack/interactivity
# and set the app's signing secret so we can verify those requests:
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=...

# ─── Engineering integrations (Phase 9) ─────────────────────
# Cloud holds creds; self-host BYO. All three providers share the same
# pattern: workspace-level install, manual ticket create from a thread,
# webhook status sync. AI drafting (Cloud + AISTACK_API_KEY) pulls
# recent ticket titles from the target + the GitHub repo's README +
# top-level tree (if installed) for voice context.
#
# Optional CRUMB_OAUTH_STATE_SECRET overrides the shared HMAC key used
# for OAuth state signing (falls back to CRUMB_INBOUND_SECRET, then any
# of the provider client secrets).
# CRUMB_OAUTH_STATE_SECRET=...

# Linear (OAuth 2.0). Register at linear.app/settings/api → Developer.
# Redirect URL: {dashboard origin}/api/integrations/linear/callback
# LINEAR_CLIENT_ID=...
# LINEAR_CLIENT_SECRET=...
# LINEAR_REDIRECT_URL=https://your-dashboard.example.com/api/integrations/linear/callback   # optional, only if x-forwarded-host is unreliable
# LINEAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=...   # configured at the Linear app's Webhooks page

# Jira (Atlassian Cloud 3LO). Register at developer.atlassian.com.
# Redirect URL: {dashboard origin}/api/integrations/jira/callback
# Scopes: read:jira-work, write:jira-work, read:jira-user, offline_access
# (+ manage:jira-webhook on Cloud, where each connection registers its own
# status webhook; self-host uses the manual webhook below)
# JIRA_CLIENT_ID=...
# JIRA_CLIENT_SECRET=...
# JIRA_REDIRECT_URL=https://your-dashboard.example.com/api/integrations/jira/callback   # optional
# JIRA_WEBHOOK_SECRET=...   # configured on the Atlassian webhook

# GitHub App (per-installation, not OAuth user). Register at
# github.com/settings/apps. Webhook URL: {dashboard origin}/api/integrations/github/webhook
# Callback URL:  {dashboard origin}/api/integrations/github/callback
# Permissions: Issues (R/W), Contents (R, for README + tree), Metadata (R)
# Events: Issues
# GITHUB_APP_ID=12345
# GITHUB_APP_SLUG=crumb-dashboard
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=...
# Recommended: the App's client ID + a client secret, with "Request user
# authorization (OAuth) during installation" turned on. Crumb then checks the
# person installing can access the installation; without them it only accepts
# installs made in the last 10 minutes (reconnecting an older one needs a
# reinstall).
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=Iv1.xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=...

# ─── CRM sync (accounts + ARR, one-way) ─────────────────────
# Cloud holds creds; self-host BYO. Refreshed by the crm-sync cron (below).
# HubSpot: public app with scopes crm.objects.companies.read + oauth.
# Redirect URL: {dashboard origin}/api/integrations/hubspot/callback
# HUBSPOT_CLIENT_ID=...
# HUBSPOT_CLIENT_SECRET=...
# Admins pick the field that holds ARR after connecting. Self-host can preset
# HubSpot's with a custom property holding what each company pays you (not
# annualrevenue, which is the company's own revenue):
# HUBSPOT_ARR_PROPERTY=arr
# HUBSPOT_REDIRECT_URL=https://your-dashboard.example.com/api/integrations/hubspot/callback   # optional
# Salesforce: connected app with scopes api + refresh_token.
# Redirect URL: {dashboard origin}/api/integrations/salesforce/callback
# SALESFORCE_CLIENT_ID=...
# SALESFORCE_CLIENT_SECRET=...
# SALESFORCE_LOGIN_URL=https://login.salesforce.com   # default; a sandbox or My Domain host otherwise
# SALESFORCE_REDIRECT_URL=https://your-dashboard.example.com/api/integrations/salesforce/callback   # optional

# ─── Chat webhook guard ─────────────────────────────────────
# Teams and Slack webhook URLs pasted into Crumb must be https on a
# Slack/Microsoft host. Setting this to 1 allows any http(s) URL, private
# addresses included: only for an internal bridge on self-host.
# CRUMB_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_ANY=1

# ─── AI clustering + ticket drafting (Cloud only) ───────────
# When set + CRUMB_TIER=cloud, new items are auto-classified against the
# workspace's initiatives and surface as accept/dismiss suggestions in
# the inbox + thread sidebar. Vendors can also batch-cluster from the
# inbox "Cluster selected" button. The same model drafts engineering
# tickets. Talks to an OpenAI-compatible aistack endpoint.
# AISTACK_API_KEY=sk-aistack-xxxxxxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# AISTACK_BASE_URL=https://aistack.eissa.cloud/v1   # optional; this is the default
# AISTACK_MODEL=qwen-35b-8bit                       # optional; default qwen-35b-8bit (128K ctx)
#
# Embeddings power semantic dedup, "Ask your feedback", and churn retrieval.
# Served at the same aistack host (returns 1024-dim, matching the pgvector
# column), so it works out of the box once AISTACK_API_KEY is set — no extra
# config. Override ONLY if the embedding model is served elsewhere:
# AISTACK_EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B   # optional; this is the default
# EMBEDDINGS_BASE_URL=https://aistack.eissa.cloud/v1  # optional; defaults to AISTACK_BASE_URL
#
# Similarity (0 to 1) at or above which two items count as the same request:
# CRUMB_DEDUP_THRESHOLD=0.85
#
# Autopilot: the "new and relevant" gate for tickets and calls pulled from
# feedback sources (Gong / Zendesk / Intercom / Freshdesk / Freshchat; their
# credentials are entered in Settings → Integrations). All 0 to 1:
# CRUMB_AUTOPILOT_MERGE_THRESHOLD=0.9      # attach to an existing item at or above this similarity
# CRUMB_AUTOPILOT_SUGGEST_THRESHOLD=0.85   # flag an existing item as a likely match
# CRUMB_AUTOPILOT_RELEVANCE_FLOOR=0.3      # drop records scored below this as not feedback
# CRUMB_AUTOPILOT_PROMOTE_RELEVANCE=0.6    # a new record needs both of these
# CRUMB_AUTOPILOT_PROMOTE_CONFIDENCE=0.6   # to become an item without review

# ─── Cost controls (Cloud) ──────────────────────────────────
# Per-workspace MONTHLY caps, metered in the usage_counters table and reset
# on the 1st (UTC) automatically. Defaults are per-plan (see lib/usage.ts);
# these env vars override the paid-plan defaults across the board.
#   AI ops/month (clustering + ticket drafts):   team 2000, growth 10000
# CRUMB_AI_MONTHLY_CAP=10000
#   Replay storage MB/month (growth only):        5120 (5 GB)
# CRUMB_REPLAY_MONTHLY_MB=5120
#   Usage events ingested/month:                  team 500000, growth 2000000
# CRUMB_USAGE_EVENTS_MONTHLY_CAP=2000000
#
# Plan-aware replay RETENTION (prunes sessions older than N days via the
# cleanup endpoint below). Default 30 days; 0 keeps replays forever. Global
# window + optional per-plan overrides (…_GROWTH / _TEAM / _FREE), which win:
# CRUMB_REPLAY_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# CRUMB_REPLAY_RETENTION_DAYS_GROWTH=90
#
# Days usage events are kept before the cleanup endpoint drops them:
# CRUMB_USAGE_EVENTS_RETENTION_DAYS=180

# ─── Billing + signup (Cloud edition only) ──────────────────
# Stripe-direct subscriptions; see DEPLOY.md "Stripe billing" for the prices,
# lookup keys and webhook ({origin}/api/v1/stripe/webhook).
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
# STRIPE_PORTAL_RETURN_URL=https://app.yourdomain.com/settings/billing   # optional; this path is the default
# Cloudflare Turnstile on the public /signup form. Unset = not enforced.
# TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=...
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=...

# ─── Maintenance / cron ─────────────────────────────────────
# Shared secret for the three internal endpoints; each 503s until it's set.
# POST to them with the header below from any cron (host crontab, k8s
# CronJob, GitHub Actions; DEPLOY.md has ready-made crontab lines):
#   replay-sweep   hourly: prunes orphaned replay sessions + uploads never
#                  linked to a reply, enforces replay retention, drops aged
#                  usage events
#   crm-sync       every few hours, if a CRM is connected
#   feedback-sync  every 15-30 minutes, if a feedback source is connected
#   curl -X POST -H "X-Crumb-Sweep-Secret: $CRUMB_INTERNAL_SWEEP_SECRET" \
#     https://your-crumb-host/api/v1/internal/replay-sweep
# CRUMB_INTERNAL_SWEEP_SECRET=...

# ─── Inbound email (reply-by-email + forward-to-capture) ────
# CRUMB_INBOUND_DOMAIN is the domain your mail provider routes to the inbound
# webhooks (Resend Inbound / SendGrid Parse / Mailgun / Postmark → POST JSON).
# Two flows:
#   - reply-by-email: a customer replies to a notification email and it lands
#     on the thread (reply+<FB-id>.<token>@DOMAIN → /api/v1/inbound/reply).
#   - forward-to-capture: forward any customer email to
#     inbox+<workspace-slug>.<token>@DOMAIN → a pending capture in /captures
#     (on Cloud, an AI account suggestion is attached for one-click confirm).
# CRUMB_INBOUND_SECRET signs those addresses (HMAC) and optionally gates the
# webhook (Authorization: Bearer <secret>). Generate: openssl rand -hex 32.
# CRUMB_INBOUND_DOMAIN=reply.yourdomain.com
# CRUMB_INBOUND_SECRET=...

# ─── Docker only ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The container runs migrations on every start; set 1 to skip them.
# CRUMB_SKIP_MIGRATIONS=1
